00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.180 align:start position:0%
the Calabar burrowing python might be  the toughest snake on earth it has  incredibly thick skin that is very  resistant to punctures these snakes have  an odd shape their heads and tails look  very similar this is thought to be a  tactic to fool predators when threatened  the Calabar hides its head and offers  its tail Calabar snakes grow to about 1  metre in length when compared with 13  other snakes of about the same size  Calabar snakes had skin that was 15  times as thick on top of this numerical  superiority the deep dermis is composed  of neatly arranged collagen bundles more  like the tough dermis of a white rhino  than its thin skin 2 snake brethren the  hinges between the Calabar scales and  its body are also specialized hinges are  thought to be useful for flexibility but  could be considered a weak spot in a  snake's armor the Calla bars hinges are  much smaller than other species all this  adds up to these snakes being very  resistant to punctures when tested with  hypodermic needles and a force  transducer its skin was orders of  magnitude harder to pierce than any  other snake tested why might a snake  need such a tough hide after all other  snakes that have shared behaviors like  slithering through leaf litter and tails  that look like their heads don't have  such a durable dermis but the Calabar  has a hunting habit that might be the  answer the team thinks the snake's armor  evolved to ward off bites from the  mothers of the baby burrowing rodents it  preys upon even with all these anti  puncture adaptations the snake's skin  remains flexible a combination that  already has piqued the interest of a  pharmaceutical company seeking to make  tougher medical gloves that don't  restrict movement  you  you  you
 

